# Auto detect text files and perform LF normalization
* text=auto

# ...but never for scripts that a Linux kernel has to exec.
#
# `* text=auto` above hands these CRLF on a Windows checkout. Git's own copy stays LF
# (mvnw is 100755 + LF in the index), so CI — which checks out on Linux — never sees a
# problem. A Docker build does: its context is the WORKING TREE, so the container gets
# `#!/bin/sh\r`, the kernel goes looking for an interpreter literally named "/bin/sh\r",
# and the build dies with the very misleading `./mvnw: not found` (exit 127).
#
# After changing this file, re-materialise the affected paths so the working tree
# actually picks the rule up — editing .gitattributes alone changes nothing on disk:
#   git rm --cached -r . && git reset --hard        (whole tree)
#   rm apps/api-java/mvnw && git checkout -- apps/api-java/mvnw   (just this one)
mvnw text eol=lf
gradlew text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf

# Binary-ish wrapper payloads: never touch them.
*.jar binary
